This PR introduces API's for fine-grained notifications as well as support for both Flows and Changelisteners.

## Kotlin API

### We need to support the following:

* Be consumable from Flows and Changelisteners.

* Have a strategy or API for handling the following states:
	- INITIAL: First callback when registering a Flow. It serves as a "baseline" for all other notifications. This is relevant as the state of the notifier Realm might also be different than the frozen Realm. Also relevant when observing changes to queries of the form: `id == <id>` where the object might be deleted and recreated.
	- UPDATED: Default "change". With fine-grained information.
	- DELETED: The object itself or the parent object was deleted. Not applicable for Realm changes.
	- ERROR: If an error happened on the Notifier thread. 

* Support for Realm, RealmList, RealmResults, RealmObject, and later RealmMap and RealmSet.

### API Suggestion

I'm proposing the following API for Kotlin fine-grained change notifications. I'm just showing List and Object notifications here. But the PR itself contains the code for Realm, Map and Set listeners as well. 


```
// List changes API
sealed interface ListChange<T: List<RealmObject>> {
    enum class State {
        INITIAL,
        UPDATED,
        DELETED,
    }
    data class Range(
        val startIndex: Int,
        val length: Int
    )
    val state: State
    val list: T
}
interface InitialList<T: List<RealmObject>>: ListChange<T>
interface UpdatedList<T: List<RealmObject>>: ListChange<T> {
    val deletions: IntArray
    val insertions: IntArray
    val changes: IntArray
    val deletionRanges: Array<Range>
    val insertionRanges: Array<Range>
    val changeRanges: Array<Range>
}
interface DeletedList<T: List<RealmObject>>: ListChange<T>


// List changes usage

// Variant 1: Use fine-grained changes
viewModelScope.launch {
	realm.filter<Person>().asFlow()
		.collect { it: ListChange<RealmResults<Person>> ->
			when(it) {
				is DeletedList -> setList(it.list) // Set the empty list
				is InitialList -> setList(it.list) 
				is UpdatedList -> updateList(it.list, it.changeRanges, it.deletionRanges, it. insertationRanges)
			}
		}
}

// Variant 2: Just show changes
viewModelScope.launch {
	realm.filter<Person>().asFlow()
		.collect { it: ListChange<RealmResults<Person>> ->
			setList(it.list) // Deleted results are treated as "empty"
		}
}


// Variant 3: Changelisteners
val token = realm.filter<Person>().addChangeListener { it: ListChange<RealmResults<Person>> ->
	// Callback on Notifier Thread
	// TODO: Consider always doing callback on UI thread, also to prevent blocking Notifier
	postOnUiThread {
		when (it) {
			is DeletedList -> setList(it.list) // Set the empty list
			is InitialList -> setList(it.list) 
			is UpdatedList -> updateList(it.list, it.changeRanges, it.deletionRanges, it. insertationRanges)
		}
	}
}

// Stop change listener. Very different API than Java, but similar to other SDK's and RxJava Disposable.
token.cancel() 

// ----------------

// Object changes API

sealed interface ObjChange<O: RealmObject> {
    enum class State {
        INITIAL,
        UPDATED,
        DELETED
    }
    val state: State
    val obj: O?
}
interface InitialObject<O: RealmObject> : ObjChange<O> {
    override val obj: O 
}
interface UpdatedObject<O: RealmObject> : ObjChange<O> {
    override val obj: O
    val changedFields: Array<String>
    fun isFieldChanged(fieldName: String): Boolean {
        return changedFields.firstOrNull { it == fieldName} != null
    }
}
interface DeletedObject<O: RealmObject> : ObjChange<O>

// Object changes usage
// Variant 1: Use fine-grained changes
viewModelScope.launch {
	realm.filter<Person>().first().asFlow()
		.collect { it: ObjectChange<Person> ->
			when(it) {
				is DeletedObject -> removeObject() // it.obj return `null`
				is InitialObject -> setObject(it.obj) 
				is UpdatedObject -> updateObject(it.obj, it.changedFields)
			}
		}
}

// Variant 2: Just show changes
viewModelScope.launch {
	realm.filter<Person>().first().asFlow()
		.collect { it: ObjectChange<Person> ->
			setObject(it.obj)
		}
}

// Variant 3: Changelisteners
val token = realm.filter<Person>().first().addChangeListener { it: ObjectChange<Person> ->
	// Callback on Notifier Thread
	// TODO: Consider always doing callback on UI thread, also to prevent blocking Notifier
	postOnUiThread {
		when (it) {
			is DeletedObject -> removeObject() // it.obj return `null`
			is InitialObject -> setObject(it.obj) 
			is UpdatedObject -> updateObject(it.obj, it.changedFields)
		}
	}
}
```

The API has the following properties:

- Changelisteners and Flows both emit 1 object, making the API between them similar. 
- These objects are sealed interfaces meaning that each class contain only relevant information.. Kotlin `when` syntax allows for seamless casting.
- We now only have one changelistener API, not two, like in Java.
- Errors are not exposed. Any error in the Notifier will crash the app. This is deemed acceptable as errors there would be unexpected and most likely unrecoverable anyway.
- Having sealed interfaces for each change type allows much better flexibility in terms of extending the API. 
- Having the observed object in the top-level interface allows for easy access if you don't care about the specific states. This introduces a few oddities like returning an empty list if the parent is deleted, but this seems acceptable.

This approach has some downsides:

- Sealed interfaces are slightly hidden. People need to know how to use them.
- The feature doesn't exist in Java. Consuming from Java properly requires extra work to be "nice".
- Unclear if exposing both a `State` property and sealed interface is overkill? The primary use case was better interop with Java and made it more clear that you need to cast the top-level interface to access fine-grained changes.

## History

*Java API:*

```
public interface OrderedRealmCollectionChangeListener<T> {
    void onChange(@Nullable T t, @Nullable OrderedCollectionChangeSet changeSet);
}

public interface OrderedCollectionChangeSet {
    enum State {
        INITIAL,
        UPDATE,
        ERROR
    }
    State getState();
    int[] getDeletions();
    int[] getInsertions();
    int[] getChanges();
    Range[] getDeletionRanges();
    Range[] getInsertionRanges();
    Range[] getChangeRanges();
    @Nullable
    Throwable getError();
    class Range {
        public final int startIndex;
        public final int length;
    }
}

public void addChangeListener(RealmChangeListener<RealmResults<E>> listener)
public void addChangeListener(OrderedRealmCollectionChangeListener<RealmResults<E>> listener) {
public Observable<CollectionChange<RealmResults<E>>> asChangesetObservable()
```

### Reasons/Behaviour

* Two addChangeListeners, as fine-grained listeners were not initially available and we didn't want to break existing users.
* `OrderedRealmCollectionChangeListener` was chosen because it was uncertain how many different types of collections we would have. There is also prior art here: https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~pattis/15-1XX/common/classes/doc/edu/cmu/cs/pattis/cs151xx/orderedCollections/OrderedCollection.html. But probably overkill and making the name overly hard to read.
* Observables/Flows only allow emitting one object. So `CollectionChange` was added for those, but it means that Flows and change listeners have different signatures and behaviors.
* The Error State was only for Query-based Sync. Other errors would throw exceptions.
* `Deleted` state was represented by either completing Flow or returning `null` in change listener.

